        "title": "経験: 〜たことがある",
        "explanationMarkdown": "## Experience with 〜たことがある\n\nIn Japanese, experience is often expressed using the pattern **verb (past form) + ことがある**. This structure indicates that someone has had the experience of doing something at least once in their life. It is commonly used when talking about life experiences rather than specific events with a clear time reference.\n\n### Basic Structure\n\n`Verb (た-form) + ことがある`\n\nThe た-form (past form) of the verb is required because the action must already have happened at some point in the past.\n\n### Negative Form\n\n`Verb (た-form) + ことがない`\n\nUse this form to say that you have never had the experience.\n\n### Asking About Experience\n\nUse the same structure in question form to ask whether someone has had an experience.\n\n### Important Usage Notes\n\n1. Do not use a specific time expression such as \"yesterday\" or \"last year\" with this pattern, because the grammar focuses on general experience.\n2. Frequency can be added with expressions like:\n- 一度 (once)\n- 二回 (twice)\n- 日本へ二回行ったことがあります。 means \"I have been to Japan twice.\"\n\nOverall, **〜たことがある** is one of the most common ways to talk about life experiences in Japanese and is similar to the English present perfect \"have done.\"",
        "examples": [
